1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:05,180
Eleven at night.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:07,421
You're in bed, and you are exhausted.

3
00:00:08,021 --> 00:00:09,281
Your alarm is set for seven.

4
00:00:09,661 --> 00:00:10,582
You know you should sleep.

5
00:00:11,082 --> 00:00:12,002
So you pick up your phone.

6
00:00:12,542 --> 00:00:13,562
Just to check one thing.

7
00:00:14,343 --> 00:00:14,883
One video.

8
00:00:15,303 --> 00:00:15,803
Then another.

9
00:00:16,343 --> 00:00:17,583
Then a message, then a feed.

10
00:00:18,064 --> 00:00:19,164
Your eyes are burning.

11
00:00:19,824 --> 00:00:21,524
You tell yourself, just one more.

12
00:00:22,285 --> 00:00:23,145
You look up at the clock.

13
00:00:23,565 --> 00:00:24,885
It's one forty in the morning.

14
00:00:25,486 --> 00:00:27,286
The next day, you drag yourself through class

15
00:00:27,326 --> 00:00:27,726
or work.

16
00:00:28,086 --> 00:00:28,606
And that night?

17
00:00:29,026 --> 00:00:30,307
You do it all over again.

18
00:00:30,867 --> 00:00:32,387
You're not even enjoying it that much.

19
00:00:33,268 --> 00:00:34,888
So why can't you just put the phone

20
00:00:34,928 --> 00:00:35,068
down?

21
00:00:35,588 --> 00:00:36,489
If this sounds like you,

22
00:00:36,529 --> 00:00:37,609
you're far from alone.

23
00:00:38,310 --> 00:00:40,431
In twenty fourteen, researchers in the Netherlands

24
00:00:40,531 --> 00:00:41,151
gave it a name.

25
00:00:41,752 --> 00:00:43,033
Bedtime procrastination.

26
00:00:43,453 --> 00:00:45,234
It means going to bed later than you

27
00:00:45,274 --> 00:00:47,275
planned, when nothing is actually stopping

28
00:00:47,315 --> 00:00:47,415
you.

29
00:00:48,035 --> 00:00:48,896
No baby crying.

30
00:00:49,236 --> 00:00:49,917
No deadline.

31
00:00:50,237 --> 00:00:52,158
Just you, and a glowing screen.

32
00:00:52,738 --> 00:00:53,759
Let's collect some clues.

33
00:00:54,579 --> 00:00:55,040
Clue one.

34
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:58,041
People who delay their bedtime often agree

35
00:00:58,181 --> 00:00:59,202
with a simple sentence.

36
00:00:59,883 --> 00:01:01,143
I want to go to bed on time,

37
00:01:01,464 --> 00:01:02,644
but I just don't.

38
00:01:03,845 --> 00:01:05,886
In surveys, putting off bedtime was linked

39
00:01:05,906 --> 00:01:08,548
to sleeping too little and feeling tired the

40
00:01:08,588 --> 00:01:08,988
next day.

41
00:01:09,689 --> 00:01:11,030
But the researchers noticed something.

42
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:13,391
People weren't saying they didn't want to

43
00:01:13,451 --> 00:01:13,711
sleep.

44
00:01:14,392 --> 00:01:16,033
They seemed to struggle with stopping whatever

45
00:01:16,053 --> 00:01:16,573
they were doing.

46
00:01:17,093 --> 00:01:17,493
Clue two.

47
00:01:18,053 --> 00:01:19,954
Another team sat down with seventeen people

48
00:01:20,014 --> 00:01:21,315
who often went to bed late.

49
00:01:21,855 --> 00:01:22,635
They asked them why.

50
00:01:23,436 --> 00:01:24,496
Some stayed up on purpose.

51
00:01:25,377 --> 00:01:26,757
Evening was the only time that felt like

52
00:01:26,797 --> 00:01:27,177
their own.

53
00:01:27,998 --> 00:01:29,639
Some simply lost track of time.

54
00:01:30,399 --> 00:01:32,140
They were absorbed, and suddenly it was two

55
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:32,620
in the morning.

56
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:35,181
And a few waited until they felt really

57
00:01:35,261 --> 00:01:37,602
sleepy, so they wouldn't lie awake in bed.

58
00:01:38,162 --> 00:01:38,642
Clue three.

59
00:01:39,483 --> 00:01:41,163
Many phones have a night mode that makes

60
00:01:41,223 --> 00:01:43,264
the screen warmer and less blue.

61
00:01:43,885 --> 00:01:45,866
In twenty twenty-one, a study tested it on

62
00:01:46,106 --> 00:01:48,447
one hundred sixty-seven young adults.

63
00:01:49,267 --> 00:01:51,208
For a week, some used their phone before

64
00:01:51,268 --> 00:01:52,769
bed with night mode on.

65
00:01:53,289 --> 00:01:54,990
Some used it with night mode off.

66
00:01:55,610 --> 00:01:57,031
And some didn't use a phone at all.

67
00:01:57,811 --> 00:01:59,412
Their sleep was tracked with a band on

68
00:01:59,432 --> 00:01:59,832
the wrist.

69
00:02:00,492 --> 00:02:01,373
Across the whole group,

70
00:02:01,813 --> 00:02:04,154
sleep looked about the same in all three

71
00:02:04,214 --> 00:02:04,514
groups.

72
00:02:05,194 --> 00:02:05,694
Clue four.

73
00:02:06,234 --> 00:02:07,375
In a brain scanning study,

74
00:02:07,395 --> 00:02:09,115
some young adults stayed awake all night.

75
00:02:09,856 --> 00:02:11,356
The next day, they rated a series of

76
00:02:11,416 --> 00:02:12,377
pictures in the scanner.

77
00:02:13,097 --> 00:02:14,217
In the sleep-deprived group,

78
00:02:14,237 --> 00:02:17,138
the brain's reward areas reacted more strongly

79
00:02:17,198 --> 00:02:18,139
to pleasant pictures.

80
00:02:19,079 --> 00:02:19,679
At the same time,

81
00:02:20,039 --> 00:02:22,580
those reward areas were less linked to regions

82
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:23,580
at the front of the brain.

83
00:02:24,401 --> 00:02:26,501
Those front regions help you weigh choices

84
00:02:26,541 --> 00:02:27,382
and put on the brakes.

85
00:02:28,282 --> 00:02:29,823
And the tired group judged more of the

86
00:02:29,863 --> 00:02:30,803
pictures as pleasant.

87
00:02:31,383 --> 00:02:32,804
So why do you stay up on your

88
00:02:32,844 --> 00:02:34,506
phone, even when you're exhausted?

89
00:02:35,566 --> 00:02:37,788
A: the blue light from the screen keeps

90
00:02:37,828 --> 00:02:38,589
your brain awake.

91
00:02:39,109 --> 00:02:41,171
B: you're not avoiding sleep,

92
00:02:41,211 --> 00:02:43,853
you're avoiding stopping, and a tired brain

93
00:02:43,893 --> 00:02:44,914
is bad at stopping.

94
00:02:45,735 --> 00:02:48,317
Or C: your phone is as addictive as

95
00:02:48,377 --> 00:02:50,018
a drug and has hijacked your brain.

96
00:02:50,659 --> 00:02:52,414
Pause the video and take a guess.

97
00:03:01,038 --> 00:03:01,700
The key is this.

98
00:03:02,381 --> 00:03:04,245
Going to sleep isn't something that just happens

99
00:03:04,305 --> 00:03:05,327
to you when you're tired.

100
00:03:05,387 --> 00:03:06,329
It's a decision.

101
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:08,073
A decision to stop.

102
00:03:09,035 --> 00:03:11,059
And at night, three things are working against

103
00:03:11,099 --> 00:03:11,660
that decision.

104
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:14,141
The first force is time that feels like

105
00:03:14,182 --> 00:03:14,522
yours.

106
00:03:15,442 --> 00:03:16,323
Think about a busy day.

107
00:03:16,843 --> 00:03:19,365
Classes, work, chores, people who need something

108
00:03:19,405 --> 00:03:19,705
from you.

109
00:03:20,426 --> 00:03:22,527
For many people, the only free hours come

110
00:03:22,548 --> 00:03:23,348
at the very end.

111
00:03:24,069 --> 00:03:25,610
Going to sleep means ending them.

112
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:27,071
So you stretch them,

113
00:03:27,311 --> 00:03:28,172
a little at a time.

114
00:03:29,132 --> 00:03:32,495
Online, people call this revenge bedtime procrastination,

115
00:03:33,055 --> 00:03:34,456
taking back the day you didn't get.

116
00:03:35,277 --> 00:03:37,098
It's the on-purpose group from clue two.

117
00:03:37,678 --> 00:03:40,399
The second force is that nothing ever ends.

118
00:03:41,159 --> 00:03:42,219
A book has a last page.

119
00:03:42,699 --> 00:03:44,079
A TV show used to end at the

120
00:03:44,119 --> 00:03:44,419
hour.

121
00:03:45,039 --> 00:03:46,520
But a feed has no bottom.

122
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:49,200
And the next video starts before you decide.

123
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:50,781
So what makes people stop?

124
00:03:51,601 --> 00:03:53,641
In one study, researchers followed forty-six

125
00:03:53,701 --> 00:03:55,141
people's scrolling for a week.

126
00:03:56,042 --> 00:03:58,162
Most reasons for stopping came from outside

127
00:03:58,202 --> 00:03:58,542
the app.

128
00:03:59,182 --> 00:04:00,002
Life got in the way.

129
00:04:00,623 --> 00:04:01,783
A message, a place to be,

130
00:04:01,863 --> 00:04:02,623
something else to do.

131
00:04:03,543 --> 00:04:04,463
Late at night, in bed,

132
00:04:04,503 --> 00:04:05,964
those interruptions rarely come.

133
00:04:06,544 --> 00:04:08,344
In another study, a reminder popped up after

134
00:04:08,404 --> 00:04:09,644
fifteen minutes of scrolling.

135
00:04:10,424 --> 00:04:12,925
People noticed it, but they often kept scrolling

136
00:04:12,985 --> 00:04:13,325
anyway.

137
00:04:13,845 --> 00:04:16,707
The third force is the tired brain from

138
00:04:16,848 --> 00:04:17,508
clue four.

139
00:04:18,229 --> 00:04:20,150
Remember, sleep loss turned up the brain's

140
00:04:20,190 --> 00:04:21,291
response to reward.

141
00:04:21,772 --> 00:04:23,373
And it loosened the link to the regions

142
00:04:23,413 --> 00:04:24,654
that help you hold back.

143
00:04:25,275 --> 00:04:26,896
That's like more gas and less brake.

144
00:04:27,757 --> 00:04:28,998
And here's the timing problem.

145
00:04:29,638 --> 00:04:31,040
The decision to go to bed comes at

146
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:32,141
the very end of the day.

147
00:04:32,601 --> 00:04:34,242
It's exactly when you're most tired.

148
00:04:34,863 --> 00:04:36,464
So the moment you most need your brakes

149
00:04:37,005 --> 00:04:38,526
is the moment they're weakest.

150
00:04:39,206 --> 00:04:40,707
You might have heard that willpower is like

151
00:04:40,848 --> 00:04:42,849
fuel, and by night your tank is empty.

152
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:45,111
That idea was popular for years.

153
00:04:45,672 --> 00:04:47,434
But when twenty-three labs repeated a key

154
00:04:47,474 --> 00:04:49,535
experiment with over two thousand people,

155
00:04:50,016 --> 00:04:51,177
the effect almost vanished.

156
00:04:52,018 --> 00:04:54,180
So scientists still debate why self-control

157
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:55,321
feels harder at night.

158
00:04:56,001 --> 00:04:57,743
Tiredness is a strong suspect.

159
00:04:58,383 --> 00:04:59,304
A magic fuel tank,

160
00:04:59,685 --> 00:05:00,205
probably not.

161
00:05:00,745 --> 00:05:02,166
There's one more thing the phone does.

162
00:05:02,846 --> 00:05:04,207
It keeps your mind busy right up to

163
00:05:04,227 --> 00:05:05,367
the moment you close your eyes.

164
00:05:05,767 --> 00:05:07,608
A survey of young adults found that frequent

165
00:05:07,728 --> 00:05:10,089
binge-watching was linked to worse sleep.

166
00:05:10,729 --> 00:05:12,250
The link ran through a busy mind.

167
00:05:12,930 --> 00:05:14,631
People's thoughts were still racing when they

168
00:05:14,671 --> 00:05:15,271
tried to sleep.

169
00:05:15,872 --> 00:05:17,592
It wasn't their bodies that stayed wired,

170
00:05:17,933 --> 00:05:18,633
it was their heads.

171
00:05:19,393 --> 00:05:20,473
So is blue light innocent?

172
00:05:21,074 --> 00:05:21,474
Not quite.

173
00:05:22,294 --> 00:05:23,214
In one lab study,

174
00:05:23,294 --> 00:05:25,195
twelve adults read on a tablet for four

175
00:05:25,235 --> 00:05:27,656
hours before bed, five nights in a row.

176
00:05:28,436 --> 00:05:29,496
Compared with a printed book,

177
00:05:29,936 --> 00:05:32,517
they made less of the sleep hormone melatonin.

178
00:05:33,237 --> 00:05:34,578
Their body clock shifted later,

179
00:05:34,998 --> 00:05:36,518
and they took about ten minutes longer to

180
00:05:36,578 --> 00:05:37,159
fall asleep.

181
00:05:37,779 --> 00:05:38,279
That's real.

182
00:05:39,359 --> 00:05:40,920
But it's four hours of bright reading,

183
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:41,600
in a lab.

184
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:43,781
And remember the night mode study.

185
00:05:44,441 --> 00:05:46,081
In everyday life, the color of the screen

186
00:05:46,181 --> 00:05:47,762
didn't seem to be the main problem.

187
00:05:48,582 --> 00:05:49,562
Light is one piece.

188
00:05:50,063 --> 00:05:51,363
It's just not the biggest one.

189
00:05:51,883 --> 00:05:53,244
If you're a teenager or in your early

190
00:05:53,284 --> 00:05:54,806
twenties, there's another reason.

191
00:05:55,486 --> 00:05:57,648
A survey of about twenty-five thousand people

192
00:05:57,809 --> 00:05:59,750
tracked when people naturally sleep at different

193
00:05:59,810 --> 00:06:00,071
ages.

194
00:06:00,992 --> 00:06:02,033
Through the teenage years,

195
00:06:02,093 --> 00:06:04,255
the body clock drifts later and later.

196
00:06:05,015 --> 00:06:06,096
It peaks around age twenty,

197
00:06:06,216 --> 00:06:07,858
then slowly moves earlier again.

198
00:06:08,478 --> 00:06:10,020
So your body may not feel sleepy at

199
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:11,341
ten, even when you're tired.

200
00:06:12,202 --> 00:06:14,004
Combine a late body clock with an early

201
00:06:14,044 --> 00:06:16,586
alarm, and the evening gets very long.

202
00:06:17,126 --> 00:06:19,187
Does that mean phones are destroying young

203
00:06:19,227 --> 00:06:20,107
people's minds?

204
00:06:21,088 --> 00:06:22,768
That's still debated among scientists.

205
00:06:23,889 --> 00:06:26,210
One large analysis of data on over three

206
00:06:26,270 --> 00:06:28,931
hundred thousand teens found the average link

207
00:06:28,951 --> 00:06:30,871
with well-being was very small.

208
00:06:31,572 --> 00:06:34,073
Other researchers argue it's bigger for some

209
00:06:34,113 --> 00:06:34,393
people.

210
00:06:35,273 --> 00:06:36,754
Sleep is where the evidence is clearer.

211
00:06:37,314 --> 00:06:38,934
A review of twenty studies and over a

212
00:06:39,034 --> 00:06:41,015
hundred twenty thousand children and teens

213
00:06:41,495 --> 00:06:42,696
found a consistent link.

214
00:06:43,636 --> 00:06:45,297
Using a device at bedtime was tied to

215
00:06:45,337 --> 00:06:47,398
roughly double the odds of not sleeping enough.

216
00:06:48,498 --> 00:06:49,919
Even having the device in the bedroom,

217
00:06:50,019 --> 00:06:52,680
without using it, was linked to less sleep.

218
00:06:53,560 --> 00:06:54,680
These studies show links,

219
00:06:55,161 --> 00:06:56,221
not proof of cause.

220
00:06:57,001 --> 00:06:58,302
But the pattern keeps showing up.

221
00:06:59,322 --> 00:07:00,243
So the answer is B.

222
00:07:00,783 --> 00:07:02,104
You're not really avoiding sleep.

223
00:07:02,504 --> 00:07:04,425
You're avoiding the end of your day.

224
00:07:05,145 --> 00:07:06,566
And you make that choice when your brain

225
00:07:06,606 --> 00:07:08,687
is tired, in front of a screen with

226
00:07:08,767 --> 00:07:09,607
no last page.

227
00:07:10,448 --> 00:07:11,769
Blue light plays a small part.

228
00:07:12,149 --> 00:07:14,170
And calling it a drug addiction is too

229
00:07:14,230 --> 00:07:14,510
simple.

230
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:16,531
So how do you make stopping easier?

231
00:07:17,231 --> 00:07:19,111
Don't count on willpower at one in the

232
00:07:19,151 --> 00:07:19,431
morning.

233
00:07:19,972 --> 00:07:21,052
Make the decision earlier.

234
00:07:21,612 --> 00:07:23,053
Charge your phone outside your bedroom,

235
00:07:23,093 --> 00:07:24,813
so stopping happens by default.

236
00:07:25,673 --> 00:07:27,334
Set an alarm that says it's time for

237
00:07:27,374 --> 00:07:29,295
bed, not just time to wake up.

238
00:07:30,015 --> 00:07:31,835
Pick your stopping point before you start.

239
00:07:32,516 --> 00:07:34,356
This episode, then lights out.

240
00:07:35,036 --> 00:07:36,397
And try to find some time for yourself

241
00:07:36,597 --> 00:07:37,337
earlier in the day.

242
00:07:38,277 --> 00:07:39,118
In a small trial,

243
00:07:39,518 --> 00:07:41,218
thirty-eight people stopped using their phones

244
00:07:41,338 --> 00:07:43,219
thirty minutes before bed for four weeks.

245
00:07:44,079 --> 00:07:45,740
Compared with a group that kept their habits,

246
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:48,341
they reported falling asleep faster and sleeping

247
00:07:48,381 --> 00:07:48,701
longer.

248
00:07:49,341 --> 00:07:50,181
It's a small study.

249
00:07:50,761 --> 00:07:52,602
But it's an easy experiment to try on

250
00:07:52,642 --> 00:07:53,002
yourself.

251
00:07:53,522 --> 00:07:54,123
Eleven at night.

252
00:07:54,783 --> 00:07:56,364
Your phone is charging in the hallway.

253
00:07:56,845 --> 00:07:57,585
You lie in bed,

254
00:07:57,605 --> 00:07:58,686
a little bored.

255
00:07:59,327 --> 00:08:00,628
You think about checking one thing.

256
00:08:01,688 --> 00:08:02,749
But it's way over there.

257
00:08:03,410 --> 00:08:05,111
Ten minutes later, you're asleep.

258
00:08:05,631 --> 00:08:06,653
This video is for learning,

259
00:08:06,713 --> 00:08:07,614
not medical advice.

260
00:08:08,536 --> 00:08:11,080
If poor sleep, low mood or worry last

261
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:13,063
for weeks, talk to a doctor or a

262
00:08:13,103 --> 00:08:13,584
counselor.

263
00:08:14,385 --> 00:08:14,946
Sweet dreams.

264
00:08:15,607 --> 00:08:16,529
And put the phone down.
